# PHOLD offsite GPU bundle

This folder contains everything needed to run the **`phold proteins-predict`** step of the metagear `structures` workflow on a GPU server, without Nextflow. After running, copy the outputs back to the source machine and re-invoke `metagear structures` to finish the workflow on CPU.

The split is deliberate: only ProstT5 inference (PHOLD_PREDICT) is GPU-heavy. The downstream Foldseek search (PHOLD_COMPARE) is fast on CPU and stays in the Nextflow pipeline.

---

## What's in this folder

| File / dir | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `README.md` | This document |
| `shards/` | The protein FASTA shards to predict on (`__NUM_SHARDS__` files, ~`__SHARD_SIZE__` proteins each) |
| `viral_join_table.tsv` | Auxiliary table consumed by the *resume* phase on the source machine. Carry-through only; not used here. |
| `submit_phold_predict.sbatch` | **SLURM batch script** — the recommended way to run on a cluster. Edit the paths at the top, then `sbatch submit_phold_predict.sbatch` |
| `run_phold_predict.sh` | The underlying runner that loops over shards. Invoked by the sbatch script; can also be run directly inside an interactive allocation or on a personal workstation |
| `manifest.json` | List of expected outputs — used by the source machine to validate completeness on resume |

**Total size:** approximately `__BUNDLE_SIZE_MB__` MB.

---

## Quick start (if you know what you're doing)

```bash
# 1. Install the env (one-time)
micromamba create -n metagear-structures -c bioconda -c conda-forge python=3.11 phold=__PHOLD_VERSION__ -y
micromamba activate metagear-structures
pip uninstall -y torch
pip install torch==2.5.1 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu121
pip install --upgrade "transformers<4.50" "numpy<2"
phold --version    # should print __PHOLD_VERSION__, no traceback

# 2. Install the PHOLD DB (one-time, ~30 min, ~13 GB; doesn't need GPU)
export HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET=1
phold install -d /persistent/path/to/phold_db -t 8

# 3. Edit submit_phold_predict.sbatch — set PHOLD_DB and verify the
#    #SBATCH partition/QoS lines match your cluster — then queue it:
sbatch submit_phold_predict.sbatch
# Job runs in the background, survives ssh disconnect, logs to phold-predict-<JOBID>.log.
# Hits walltime before finishing? Just `sbatch submit_phold_predict.sbatch` again —
# the script's --resume flag picks up where it left off, idempotently.
```

The detailed walkthrough is below. The version pins above are not optional — they sidestep three real failure modes documented in *Troubleshooting* at the end.

---

## Step 1 — Get this bundle onto the GPU server

```bash
rsync -avzP <source>/structures/offsite_predict/  <user>@<gpu-host>:/path/on/gpu-host/
```

If the GPU server shares a filesystem with the source machine (e.g. a DSS mount visible on both), no copy is needed — just `cd` into the folder on the GPU side.

---

## Step 2 — Set up the runtime (one-time)

The GPU server needs:

- **A CUDA-capable Nvidia GPU** (any card with ≥ 8 GB VRAM works; an H100/A100 finishes in a fraction of the time)
- **The `phold` binary on PATH**, with PyTorch + CUDA support
- **A complete PHOLD database installed locally** (`phold install -d <path>`, ~13 GB)

### The env install — version pins MATTER

A naïve `micromamba create … phold=__PHOLD_VERSION__` installs a CPU-only PyTorch with a transformers version that doesn't import. The recipe below is the **tested working combination**:

| Package | Version | Why pinned |
|---|---|---|
| `phold` | `__PHOLD_VERSION__` | The pipeline-matched version. |
| `torch` | `2.5.1+cu121` | CUDA-enabled build. Conda's default channel installs CPU-only. |
| `transformers` | `<4.50` | Newer transformers uses `torch.float8_e8m0fnu` (only in torch ≥ 2.7). |
| `numpy` | `<2` | phold declares `numpy<2`; numpy 2.x has ABI changes that break many ML packages. |

#### Step-by-step install (micromamba, user-space, no admin needed)

```bash
# One-time install of micromamba in $HOME
curl -Ls https://micro.mamba.pm/api/micromamba/linux-64/latest | tar -xvj bin/micromamba
./bin/micromamba shell init --shell bash --root-prefix=$HOME/micromamba
source ~/.bashrc

# 1) Create the env with phold (conda gives us the CLI deps: foldseek, mmseqs2, etc.)
#    This pulls a CPU torch as a placeholder; we replace it next.
micromamba create -n metagear-structures \
    -c bioconda -c conda-forge \
    python=3.11 \
    phold=__PHOLD_VERSION__ \
    -y

micromamba activate metagear-structures

# 2) Replace the CPU torch with a CUDA-enabled torch (PyTorch's official wheels
#    are tested per CUDA version — most reliable for GPU work)
pip uninstall -y torch torchvision torchaudio
pip install torch==2.5.1 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu121

# 3) Pin transformers + numpy to compatible versions
pip install --upgrade "transformers<4.50" "numpy<2"

# 4) Verify the import chain works
phold --version
# Expect: "phold, version __PHOLD_VERSION__"  with no traceback.

# 5) Verify torch sees CUDA support (this works on the login node;
#    actual GPU availability requires being on a compute node)
python3 -c "import torch; print('torch:', torch.__version__, '| CUDA build:', torch.version.cuda)"
# Expect: torch: 2.5.1+cu121 | CUDA build: 12.1
```

### Install the PHOLD DB (one-time, ~30 min, no GPU needed)

```bash
micromamba activate metagear-structures
export HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET=1   # see troubleshooting — needed for some environments
phold install -d /persistent/path/to/phold_db -t 8
```

This downloads:
- The ProstT5 weights (~11 GB) — large but worth caching to a persistent path
- The Foldseek structural index (~2 GB)
- PHROG annotation tables (~50 MB)

Total ~13 GB. **Put the DB on a persistent filesystem** (e.g. on LRZ-HGX, a DSS path under `/dss/dssfs02/.../databases/phold_db`) so multiple allocations / multiple users can share it.

If a DB is already in place from a previous install, just point at it.

### Alternative paths

- **NVIDIA Pyxis / Enroot:** point the bundled script at the phold container directly; the script doesn't care how phold got on PATH.
- **Singularity:** `singularity exec --nv phold-__PHOLD_VERSION__.img ./run_phold_predict.sh …` works the same.

---

## Step 3 — Submit the job (recommended path: `sbatch`)

**Why sbatch and not `salloc` + `srun`:** an `salloc`+`srun --pty bash` allocation dies the moment your ssh session disconnects. A multi-hour PHOLD run that needs to survive a laptop sleep, a flaky VPN, or a wifi handover **must** be a batch job. `sbatch` queues the work, runs it on the compute node detached from your shell, writes logs to disk, and lets you check in with `squeue` whenever you like.

### 3a. Edit `submit_phold_predict.sbatch`

Open the bundled script and adjust two things:

1. **The `#SBATCH` header** — partition, QoS, account, time — to match your cluster's conventions. The default is set up for LRZ-HGX (`-p lrz-hgx-h100-94x4 --gres=gpu:1 --time=__SUGGESTED_WALLTIME__`).
2. **The `PHOLD_DB` line** in the body — point it at the absolute path where you installed the PHOLD database in Step 2.

The script's other knobs (`BATCH_SIZE`, `THREADS`, `MAMBA_ENV`) have sensible defaults; override at submit time via env vars if you need to.

### 3b. Submit

```bash
cd /path/to/offsite_predict
sbatch submit_phold_predict.sbatch
# → Submitted batch job 1234567
```

Now disconnect, go to bed — the job is running. Logs stream to `phold-predict-1234567.log` and `phold-predict-1234567.err` in the submit directory.

### 3c. Monitor

```bash
squeue -u $USER                  # see your queued/running jobs
tail -f phold-predict-*.log      # follow live progress
scancel <JOBID>                  # if you need to stop it
```

### 3d. Re-submit after walltime / failure

LRZ-HGX caps individual allocations at **48 hours**. The `--resume` flag inside the sbatch script means a second `sbatch submit_phold_predict.sbatch` picks up exactly where the first one left off — no manual bookkeeping needed.

| Hardware | Per-shard ETA | Total walltime | Re-submissions on LRZ (48h cap) |
|---|---|---|---|
| H100 94 GB (batch=32) | 4–6 min | `__ETA_H100__` | 1 (comfortable) |
| H100 94 GB (batch=16) | 5–8 min | `__ETA_H100_B16__` | 1 |
| A100 40 GB (batch=16) | 8–12 min | `__ETA_A100__` | 1 |
| V100 16 GB (batch=8) | 12–20 min | `__ETA_V100__` | 1–2 |
| T4 16 GB / RTX 3070 8 GB (batch=2) | 25–45 min | `__ETA_T4__` | not realistic at this scale |
| CPU fallback (8 threads, batch=1) | 4–6 h | `__ETA_CPU__` | use a different cluster |

### 3e. Override knobs at submit time

```bash
# Smaller batch size for a less-powerful GPU
BATCH_SIZE=8 sbatch submit_phold_predict.sbatch

# Different DB path
PHOLD_DB=/some/other/path sbatch submit_phold_predict.sbatch

# Different env name
MAMBA_ENV=my-phold-env sbatch submit_phold_predict.sbatch
```

### Alternative: interactive / non-SLURM

If you really need an interactive shell (debugging, single-shard testing) or you're on a personal workstation without SLURM, skip the sbatch wrapper and call the underlying script directly:

```bash
# Personal workstation: just activate the env and run
micromamba activate metagear-structures
bash run_phold_predict.sh \
    --phold-db /persistent/path/to/phold_db \
    --batch-size 16 \
    --resume

# Interactive SLURM allocation (NOT recommended for long runs)
salloc -p lrz-hgx-h100-94x4 --gres=gpu:1 -c 6 --mem=60G --time=4:00:00
srun --jobid $SLURM_JOB_ID --pty bash
# ... activate env and run as above
```

`run_phold_predict.sh` itself takes `--phold-db`, `--batch-size`, `--threads`, `--resume`, `--dry-run`. See `bash run_phold_predict.sh --help` for the full list.

---

## Step 4 — Verify

After each run / final run, confirm shard coverage:

```bash
ls outputs/predict_*/phold_aa.fasta | wc -l
# Should equal __NUM_SHARDS__ when fully done
```

Spot-check one output dir for sanity:

```bash
ls outputs/predict_*/  | head -1   # pick one
# Expect each predict_<shard>/ to contain:
#   phold_aa.fasta
#   phold_3di.fasta
#   phold_prostT5_3di_mean_probabilities.csv
#   phold_prostT5_3di_all_probabilities.json
```

If some shards are missing or partial after the final allocation, re-run with `--resume` — only the missing/incomplete ones rerun.

---

## Step 5 — Ship results back

```bash
rsync -avzP outputs/  <user>@<source-host>:<outdir>/structures/phold_predict/
```

`<outdir>` is the same `--outdir` you used in the original `metagear structures` call.

---

## Step 6 — Resume on the source machine

Re-invoke metagear, dropping `--structures_prepare_for_gpu` and **explicitly passing `--phold_predict_dir`**:

```bash
metagear structures \
    --input  inputs/samplesheet.csv \
    --outdir <same-outdir-as-before> \
    --reuse-outputs \
    --structures_scope <same-scope-as-before> \
    --phold_predict_dir <outdir>/structures/phold_predict
```

The pipeline detects the on-disk predict dirs, skips PHOLD_PREDICT, and resumes from `MERGE_PHOLD_PREDICTIONS`. PHOLD_COMPARE runs on CPU (~10 min on viral_only scale, scales linearly). Final annotations land at `<outdir>/annotations/phold/`.

If any shards are missing, MERGE_PHOLD_PREDICTIONS warns about them but proceeds — PHOLD_COMPARE will fail loudly downstream if too many are missing.

---

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `AssertionError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled` | CPU-only PyTorch was installed | Run the `pip install torch==2.5.1 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu121` step from the install recipe |
| `AttributeError: module 'torch' has no attribute 'float8_e8m0fnu'` | transformers ≥ 4.50 expects torch ≥ 2.7 | `pip install "transformers<4.50"` |
| `phold __PHOLD_VERSION__ requires numpy<2, but you have numpy 2.x` (pip warning) | numpy 2.x ABI break | `pip install "numpy<2"` |
| `phold: command not found` | Env not activated | `micromamba activate metagear-structures` first |
| `CUDA not available` inside python on a compute node | Container without `--nv` flag (Singularity); allocation didn't include `--gres=gpu:1` | Verify `nvidia-smi` shows a GPU first |
| `RuntimeError: CUDA out of memory` | `batch_size` too high for VRAM | Drop to `--batch-size 8`, then `--batch-size 4`, etc. |
| `No CA certificates were loaded from the system` (from hf-xet) | Container without CA roots OR network restrictions | Script already exports `HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET=1`; if still failing, the install was incomplete — re-run `phold install` |
| Shard hangs at 0% for >5 min on first run | ProstT5 model loading + tokenizer cache | Wait — usually unstuck after ~3 min, then fast |
| Some shards finished, some failed | Mixed exit codes (OOM, walltime, network) | Use `--resume` to retry only the missing/incomplete ones |

If you hit something not on this list, save `outputs/predict_<shard>/phold_proteins-predict_*.log` from the failing shard and report back — the log captures everything PHOLD did.

---

## Notes for the future / pipeline maintainers

This README documents the **tested working combination** as of bundle creation:

- `torch==2.5.1+cu121`
- `transformers<4.50` (4.49.0 verified)
- `numpy<2` (1.x verified)
- `phold==__PHOLD_VERSION__`

If you regenerate this bundle from a newer pipeline version (or use a different phold version), revisit these pins. The `transformers` and `torch` ecosystem moves fast and version mismatches surface as cryptic import errors.
